\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

Across 100+ experiments and 6000+ evaluation episodes on two VLA model families, three findings converge: current VLA safety evaluation is structurally unreliable. Action collapse renders reported CAR illusory (\S\ref{sec:dfcar}); EE-only oracle refinement is counterproductive (\S\ref{sec:oracle}); and collision-free demonstrations, not geometric supervision, account for all measured avoidance gains (\S\ref{sec:avoidance_source}).

\paragraph{Why EE-only SDF fails.}
Two independent causes explain the null result. \textit{(i)~Demonstration data subsumes the SDF signal.} Collision-free demonstrations implicitly encode spatial avoidance; the ${\sim}$28\,pp CAR gain arises entirely from this distributional shift, with per-task ordering invariant across all SDF conditions (\S\ref{sec:avoidance_source}). At low $\lambda$, SDF is redundant; at $\lambda = 0.1$, the marginal signal preserves the same demonstration-driven pattern. \textit{(ii)~EE monitoring cannot prevent full-body collisions.} The SDF gradient acts on a single point while collisions occur along intermediate links. Oracle experiments confirm this: ground-truth SDF refinement \emph{reduces} CAR by 6--10\,pp below unrefined baselines (\S\ref{sec:oracle}), because pushing the EE away rotates unmonitored links through obstacles. These levels are independent; effective geometric safety requires both full-body supervision and training signals beyond demonstrations.

\paragraph{Implications for evaluation.}
dfCAR corrects action-collapse contamination using only EE position logging, already available in most evaluation pipelines. The bimodal displacement distribution makes $\tau_d$ robust across five orders of magnitude. We recommend reporting both CAR and dfCAR; the gap quantifies contamination. This contamination spans a 4000$\times$ parameter range (Pi0 Full-FT ${\sim}$2.5B to Pi0+A1 ${\sim}$658K trainable), confirming it is a structural consequence of evaluating avoidance without verifying motion. More broadly, safety may need to operate at the planning or joint-space constraint layer, since any EE-centric loss inherits the same blind spot.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our evaluation covers one benchmark suite (SafeLIBERO, four tasks) and two model families (Pi0, Pi0.5). The tabletop setting with fixed-base manipulator and known obstacles may not generalize to mobile platforms and cluttered scenes. Oracle experiments used a largely frozen model (Pi0+A1, TSR${\approx}$0\%); testing on an actively moving policy would better isolate the structural limitation. The $\lambda = 0.1$ condition has $n = 2$ seeds; the marginal departure ($p \approx 0.023$) is inconclusive. Full-body SDF remains untested. Fine-tuning degrades TSR by ${\sim}$21--26\,pp, yet SSR/TSR increases from 0.33 to 0.51--0.58 (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_ssr}), ruling out reduced exposure as the sole explanation.

\paragraph{Future work.}
Three directions follow: (1) full-body SDF supervision along the kinematic chain; (2) cross-architecture evaluation on OpenVLA, RT-2, and other VLA families; (3) planning-layer safety constraints in joint space rather than EE space.

\paragraph{Broader impact.}
Contaminated CAR scores allow models with no genuine avoidance to pass safety benchmarks. dfCAR exposes this gap with minimal changes to existing protocols.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Vision-Language-Action Models.}
VLA models combine pretrained vision-language backbones with action prediction for generalist manipulation~\cite{brohan2023rt2,black2024pi0,chi2023diffusionpolicy,kim2024openvla}. Recent work enhances spatial reasoning through auxiliary 3D supervision~\cite{li2025spatialforcing,li2025qdepth,depthvla2025}, but safety considerations remain largely absent: most VLAs train on collision-free demonstrations and evaluate task success alone, with no mechanism to distinguish genuine avoidance from incidental collision-free trajectories.

\paragraph{Safety in Robot Learning.}
Safety-aware approaches span training-time constrained RL~\cite{zhang2025safevla} and test-time filters: control barrier functions~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, collision risk gating~\cite{cofreeevla2026}, inverse kinematics QP~\cite{spalter2024ikinqp}, and diffusion-process collision costs~\cite{li2024langguided,yang2025cape}. A recent survey~\cite{li2026vlasafety} categorizes these into runtime shields, constrained optimization, and representation-based methods. Common to all is that safety is imposed externally rather than learned within the policy's representations. This work tests whether auxiliary SDF supervision can produce intrinsic collision awareness.

\paragraph{Signed Distance Functions in Robotics.}
SDFs encode minimum surface distance with sign indicating free space or penetration. Neural SDFs~\cite{park2019deepsdf} enable continuous shape representation; in robotics, SDFs serve perception~\cite{ortiz2022isdf}, planning~\cite{huang2023voxposer,vasilopoulos2023ramp}, and differentiable collision detection~\cite{zhi2021diffco}. Recent extensions to configuration space~\cite{chen2026cssdfnet} and model predictive control~\cite{jacquet2025neuralnmpc} demonstrate effectiveness in classical pipelines, but integration into end-to-end learned policies remains unexplored. Critically, most methods query the SDF at the end-effector only, leaving full-body collision awareness as an open problem.

\paragraph{Evaluation Metrics for Safe Manipulation.}
SafeLIBERO~\cite{hu2025vlsa} extends LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero} with obstacle-augmented environments and a collision avoidance rate (CAR) metric. However, CAR treats all collision-free episodes identically: a frozen robot scores near-perfect CAR without any avoidance behavior. This failure mode echoes concerns about metric reliability~\cite{karwowski2024goodhart,gururangan2018annotation} and binary success metrics under limited samples~\cite{snyder2026beyond}. Action collapse in diffusion policies~\cite{dong2025conditioning} systematically inflates CAR but has not been previously examined as a safety evaluation confounder. We propose dfCAR (\S\ref{sec:dfcar}) to detect this contamination.

